ACKNOWLEDGMENTS FOR EVERY xv6 PORT IN THIS REPOSITORY

Every port under forks/ descends from MIT's xv6 and inherits the same
acknowledgments, so they are kept here once rather than repeated fourteen
times.  Each fork's own README keeps only what is actually specific to it:
which machine it targets, where it borrows arch-specific code from, where
to send error reports, and how to build and run it.

Where the ports' own copies disagreed, the fullest text was taken - in
particular the contributor list below is the longest of the fourteen (the
one from the actively maintained mit-pdos/xv6-riscv), and it was checked to
be a superset of every other fork's list, so that no contributor loses
credit by this consolidation.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------

xv6 is inspired by John Lions's Commentary on UNIX 6th Edition (Peer
to Peer Communications; ISBN: 1-57398-013-7; 1st edition (June 14,
2000)).  See also https://pdos.csail.mit.edu/6.1810/, which provides
pointers to on-line resources for v6.

The following people have made contributions: Russ Cox (context switching,
locking), Cliff Frey (MP), Xiao Yu (MP), Nickolai Zeldovich, and Austin
Clements.

